## TL;DR  
Yes – you can display a **MapLibre-style vector-tile basemap** inside a Leaflet map. The easiest way is to use the tiny bridge library **`maplibre-gl-leaflet`** (or its successor `leaflet-maplibre-gl`). It lets you treat a full-blown MapLibre GL JS map as a Leaflet layer, so you keep all of Leaflet’s UI (controls, markers, pop-ups, etc.) while the background tiles are rendered by MapLibre GL.

Below is a complete, copy-and-paste-ready recipe (CDN-only) followed by a more detailed “how it works” section, alternative approaches, and a checklist of gotchas.

---

## 1️⃣ Quick-Start (CDN)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre basemap</title>

  <!-- Leaflet CSS -->
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
    integrity="sha256-sA+e2bZc+gZc+K5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c="
    crossorigin=""
  />

  <style>
    html, body, #map { height: 100%; margin: 0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<!-- Leaflet JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"
        integrity="sha256-+bZc+gZc+K5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c="
        crossorigin=""></script>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>

<!-- Bridge: maplibre-gl-leaflet (or leafet-maplibre-gl) -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the Leaflet map (any CRS, any options you like)
  const map = L.map('map', {
    center: [51.505, -0.09],
    zoom: 13,
    // optional: make the Leaflet map use the same max/min zoom as the style
    maxZoom: 22,
    minZoom: 0
  });

  // 2️⃣ Create the MapLibre GL layer
  const mapLibreLayer = L.maplibreGL({
    // URL to a MapLibre style JSON (can be local or remote)
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    // Optional: pass any MapLibre GL options you need
    // e.g. accessToken: 'YOUR_TOKEN'   // if your source needs it
    //   , attribution: '&copy; OpenMapTiles &copy; OpenStreetMap contributors'
  }).addTo(map);

  // 3️⃣ Add any Leaflet overlays you want (markers, polygons, etc.)
  L.marker([51.5, -0.09]).addTo(map)
    .bindPopup('I am a Leaflet marker on top of a MapLibre basemap')
    .openPopup();

  // 4️⃣ (Optional) Sync the cursor style for a smoother UX
  mapLibreLayer.getContainer().style.cursor = 'grab';
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Result:** A Leaflet map with a fully styled vector-tile basemap rendered by MapLibre GL, plus regular Leaflet markers, pop-ups, and controls.

---

## 2️⃣ What’s Happening Under the Hood?

| Component | Role |
|-----------|------|
| **Leaflet** | Handles the map container, UI controls, DOM-based overlays (markers, pop-ups, vector layers, etc.). |
| **MapLibre GL JS** | Renders the vector tiles on a WebGL canvas using the style JSON you provide. |
| **`maplibre-gl-leaflet`** (or `leaflet-maplibre-gl`) | A thin wrapper that creates a Leaflet `Layer` whose `onAdd`/`onRemove` methods mount/unmount a MapLibre GL map inside the Leaflet map’s pane. It also forwards Leaflet events (zoom, pan, resize) to the GL map so the two stay in sync. |

### Why Use the Bridge?

* **Full MapLibre styling** – you can use any MapLibre style (OpenMapTiles, MapTiler, custom, etc.) without having to raster-tile it yourself.
* **Leaflet UI stays familiar** – you still use `L.marker`, `L.geoJSON`, `L.control`, etc.
* **Performance** – Vector tiles are rendered on the GPU, so you get smooth zoom/pan even at high zoom levels.

---

## 3️⃣ Step-by-Step Guide (npm / bundler)

If you’re building with a bundler (Webpack, Vite, Rollup, etc.) you’ll probably want the npm versions instead of the CDN links.

```bash
# Install the three packages
npm i leaflet maplibre-gl maplibre-gl-leaflet
# (or yarn add …)
```

```js
// main.js (or wherever you initialise the map)
import L from 'leaflet';
import 'leaflet/dist/leaflet.css';

import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import 'maplibre-gl/dist/maplibre-gl.css';

import { MapLibreGL } from 'maplibre-gl-leaflet'; // the wrapper

// 1️⃣ Leaflet map
const map = L.map('map', {
  center: [40.7128, -74.0060],
  zoom: 12,
  maxZoom: 22,
  minZoom: 0
});

// 2️⃣ MapLibre GL layer
const glLayer = new MapLibreGL({
  style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY',
  // any MapLibre GL options go here
  // e.g. transformRequest: (url, resourceType) => { … }
}).addTo(map);

// 3️⃣ Leaflet overlays (markers, polygons, etc.)
L.marker([40.7128, -74.0060]).addTo(map)
  .bindPopup('Hello from Leaflet!');

// 4️⃣ Optional: keep the GL canvas on top of the tile pane but below the overlay pane
glLayer.setZIndex(1); // default is fine for most cases
```

**Note:** The wrapper exports a class called `MapLibreGL` (or `L.maplibreGL` when you import the side-effects version). Both work; pick the style you prefer.

---

## 4️⃣ Alternative Approaches (when the bridge isn’t enough)

| Approach | When to use it | Pros | Cons |
|----------|----------------|------|------|
| **`L.VectorGrid` (leaflet.vectorgrid)** | You only need a few vector layers (e.g., roads, admin boundaries) and want them as Leaflet vector objects. | Full Leaflet interactivity (feature events, styling via Canvas/SVG). | No full MapLibre style support – you have to style each layer manually. |
| **Render MapLibre GL in a separate container** | You want a completely independent GL map but still need Leaflet UI elsewhere. | Clean separation, can use MapLibre GL’s own controls. | Overlays can’t be mixed; you need to sync two maps manually. |
| **Convert style to raster tiles (e.g., via TileServer-GL, Tileserver-PHP, MapTiler Cloud)** | Your target environment can’t use WebGL (very old browsers, some mobile WebViews). | Works everywhere Leaflet works. | Loss of vector benefits (no dynamic styling, larger bandwidth). |
| **`leaflet-glify`** | You have a small amount of GeoJSON that you want to render with WebGL for performance. | Very lightweight, no external style JSON needed. | Not a full basemap solution. |

---

## 5️⃣ Gotchas & Best-Practice Checklist

| Issue | What to watch for | Fix / Recommendation |
|-------|-------------------|-----------------------|
| **CORS / Access Tokens** | Many vector tile providers (MapTiler, Mapbox, OpenMapTiles) require an API key or CORS headers. | Use a style URL that includes your token (`?key=…`) **or** host the style & tiles on the same domain (or enable CORS). |
| **Zoom limits** | Leaflet’s default `maxZoom` is 18; many vector styles go to 22. | Set `maxZoom` on the Leaflet map to match the style (`map.setMaxZoom(22)`). |
| **Attribution** | MapLibre GL does not automatically add attributions to Leaflet’s control. | Pass `attribution` in the wrapper options or add a separate `L.control.attribution`. |
| **CSS Z-index** | The GL canvas lives in its own pane; sometimes it can hide Leaflet markers. | Use `glLayer.setZIndex(1)` (or higher) and make sure your overlay pane (`overlayPane`) is above it (default). |
| **Touch gestures** | On mobile, both Leaflet and MapLibre GL listen for pinch/drag. The bridge forwards events, but double-tap zoom can be jittery. | Set `mapLibreLayer.getMap().dragRotate = false;` if you don’t need map rotation. |
| **Performance on low-end devices** | WebGL is fast, but rendering many layers (e.g., 30+ sources) can tax older GPUs. | Keep the style lean (disable unnecessary layers) or provide a raster fallback for those devices. |
| **Version compatibility** | The bridge is thin; it tracks MapLibre GL major releases. | Use matching versions: `maplibre-gl@4.x` with `maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13`. If you upgrade, check the bridge’s release notes. |
| **SSR / Server-Side Rendering** | Leaflet expects a DOM; the bridge also creates a canvas. | Guard the initialization with `if (typeof window !== 'undefined') { … }`. |
| **Custom fonts / glyphs** | Vector styles may reference glyphs that aren’t bundled. | Host the glyphs yourself or use a style that references a public glyphs URL (MapLibre’s demo style does). |

