## TL;DR  
Yes – you can display a **MapLibre-style vector-tile basemap** inside a Leaflet map.  
The easiest way is to use the **`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet`** plugin, which embeds a MapLibre GL JS canvas as a Leaflet layer.  

Below is a complete, copy-and-paste-ready example plus a step-by-step guide, alternatives, and a checklist of gotchas.

---

## 1. How it works under the hood  

| Component | What it does | Why it matters |
|-----------|--------------|----------------|
| **Leaflet** | 2-D map framework (DOM, Canvas, SVG) | Handles UI, controls, other raster/vector layers, routing, etc. |
| **MapLibre GL JS** | WebGL renderer for MapLibre style JSON + vector tiles (pbf) | Draws the basemap with the full styling power of MapLibre (layers, filters, expressions). |
| **`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet`** | Thin wrapper that creates a Leaflet `Layer` (`L.maplibreGL`) whose `onAdd`/`onRemove` mount/unmount a MapLibre GL map inside the Leaflet container. | Lets you treat the GL canvas like any other Leaflet overlay (z-order, opacity, events). |

Result: a Leaflet map that can still use its own layers (markers, polygons, heatmaps, etc.) **and** a full-featured vector-tile basemap styled with a MapLibre style JSON.

---

## 2. Minimal Working Example  

> **Prerequisites**  
> * A web server (even a simple `python -m http.server`) – Leaflet needs to load assets via HTTP.  
> * A publicly reachable **style JSON** (or a local file served with CORS).  
> * Vector tiles in the **MapLibre** format (`.pbf`).  
>   – You can use a public source like **OpenMapTiles**, **Planetiler**, **tileserver-gl**, or any XYZ vector-tile service that returns `application/x-protobuf`.  

### 2.1. HTML (index.html)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre GL basemap</title>

  <!-- Leaflet CSS -->
  <link
    rel="stylesheet"
    href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"
    integrity="sha256-sA+e2bZc+gZc+K5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c="
    crossorigin=""
  />

  <style>
    html, body, #map { height: 100%; margin: 0; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<!-- Leaflet JS -->
<script
  src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"
  integrity="sha256-+bZc+gZc+K5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c5c="
  crossorigin=""></script>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>

<!-- MapLibre-GL-Leaflet plugin -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the Leaflet map (any CRS works – we use the default EPSG:3857)
  const map = L.map('map', {
    center: [51.505, -0.09], // London
    zoom: 12,
    // optional: make the GL layer the “base” layer (no background tiles)
    preferCanvas: false
  });

  // 2️⃣ Add the MapLibre GL layer
  const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
    // 👉 Either a remote URL or a relative path to a style.json
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // demo style (OpenStreetMap)
    // If your style references a token (e.g. MapTiler), add it here:
    // accessToken: 'YOUR_MAPTILER_TOKEN',
    // Optional: set maxZoom/minZoom to match your vector tile source
    maxZoom: 18,
    minZoom: 0,
    // Optional: make the GL canvas transparent so underlying Leaflet layers show through
    // (default is false – the GL canvas clears the background each frame)
    // transparent: true,
  }).addTo(map);

  // 3️⃣ Add a regular Leaflet marker on top (just to prove layering works)
  L.marker([51.505, -0.09])
    .addTo(map)
    .bindPopup('I am a Leaflet marker!')
    .openPopup();

  // 4️⃣ (Optional) Add a Leaflet vector layer (e.g. GeoJSON) to test interaction
  const geojson = {
    "type": "FeatureCollection",
    "features": [{
      "type": "Feature",
      "properties": { "name": "Park" },
      "geometry": {
        "type": "Polygon",
        "coordinates": [[
          [-0.1, 51.51],
          [-0.08, 51.51],
          [-0.08, 51.5],
          [-0.1, 51.5],
          [-0.1, 51.51]
        ]]
      }
    }]
  };
  L.geoJSON(geojson, {
    style: {color: 'orange', weight: 2, fillOpacity: 0.2}
  }).addTo(map);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### 2.2. What you just did  

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Leaflet map** | Standard Leaflet initialization (`L.map`). |
| **`L.maplibreGL({style: …})`** | Creates a Leaflet layer that internally spins up a MapLibre GL map using the supplied style JSON. |
| **Add Leaflet overlays** | Anything you add *after* the GL layer will be drawn **on top** (markers, GeoJSON, heatmaps, etc.). |
| **`transparent: true`** (optional) | If you want the GL canvas to be transparent (e.g., you have a semi-transparent raster tile underneath), set this flag. Most basemap use-cases keep it `false`. |

