| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **1** | Created a normal Leaflet map (`L.map`). |
| **2** | Instantiated `L.maplibreGL` with a **MapLibre style URL**. The plugin creates an internal MapLibre GL map, loads the style, and draws it on a WebGL canvas that is kept in sync with Leaflet’s view. |
| **3** | Added a regular Leaflet marker to prove that Leaflet layers sit **above** the GL canvas (z-order works). |
| **4** | (Optional) Show how you could add a raster fallback for browsers that don’t support WebGL. |

---

## 3. Step-by-Step Guide for a Real Project

### 3.1. Prepare the vector-tile source & style

1. **Vector tiles**  
   * Must be in **Mapbox Vector Tile (MVT / .pbf)** format.  
   * Serve them via a tile server that supports CORS (e.g., **tileserver-gl**, **tileserver-php**, **t-rex**, **tileserver-maplibre**, **Amazon S3 + CloudFront**, **MapLibre Cloud**, etc.).  
   * URL pattern should be `https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`.

2. **Style JSON**  
   * Either write your own style (see the MapLibre Style Specification) or start from an existing open-source style (e.g., **OSM Bright**, **OpenMapTiles**, **MapLibre Demo style**).  
   * In the style, reference the vector source you just set up, e.g.:

   ```json
   {
     "version": 8,
     "sources": {
       "osm": {
         "type": "vector",
         "tiles": ["https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"],
         "maxzoom": 14
       }
     },
     "layers": [
       {
         "id": "water",
         "type": "fill",
         "source": "osm",
         "source-layer": "water",
         "paint": { "fill-color": "#a0c8f0" }
       },
       …
     ]
   }
   ```

   * Host the JSON somewhere reachable by the browser (static file on your CDN, GitHub Pages, etc.).  
   * **Tip:** If you use **OpenMapTiles** you can download a ready-made style (`openmaptiles.json`) and just replace the `url` of the `openmaptiles` source.

### 3.2. Add the required libraries

| Library | CDN (latest stable) | NPM |
|---------|--------------------|-----|
| Leaflet | `https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js` | `npm i leaflet` |
| MapLibre GL JS | `https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js` | `npm i maplibre-gl` |
| MapLibre-GL-Leaflet plugin | `https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js` | `npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet` |

> **Why the plugin?**  
> Leaflet itself cannot render vector tiles directly. The plugin gives you a **Leaflet `Layer`** that internally runs a full MapLibre GL map, so you keep all the styling power without leaving the Leaflet ecosystem.

### 3.3. Initialise the Leaflet map

```js
const map = L.map('map', {
  center: [lat, lng],
  zoom: 12,
  maxZoom: 18, // match your vector tile maxzoom
  minZoom: 5
});
```

### 3.4. Add the MapLibre GL layer

```js
const glLayer = L.maplibreGL({
  style: 'https://my.cdn.com/styles/my-style.json', // <-- your style URL
  // optional MapLibre GL options:
  // token: 'YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN', // if you use a source that needs auth
  // attributionControl: false, // Leaflet already has its own attribution
  // interactive: true, // default – lets you click features via maplibre-gl's queryRenderedFeatures
}).addTo(map);
```

### 3.5. (Optional) Sync Leaflet popups with vector features

If you want to open a Leaflet popup when a user clicks a vector feature:

```js
map.on('click', function (e) {
  // Query the MapLibre GL map that lives inside the layer
  const features = glLayer.getMap().queryRenderedFeatures(
    map.latLngToContainerPoint(e.latlng),
    { layers: ['road', 'building'] } // limit to layers you care about
  );

  if (features.length) {
    const f = features[0];
    L.popup()
      .setLatLng(e.latlng)
      .setContent(`<strong>${f.layer.id}</strong><br>${JSON.stringify(f.properties)}`)
      .openOn(map);
  }
});
```

### 3.6. Add any other Leaflet layers you need

```js
L.marker([lat, lng]).addTo(map);
L.geoJSON(myGeojson).addTo(map);
L.tileLayer('https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png', {
  attribution: '&copy; OSM contributors'
}).addTo(map);
```

All of these will appear **above** the GL basemap (unless you change the z-order with `bringToFront()` / `bringToBack()`).

---

## 4. Alternative Approaches (if you don’t want the plugin)

| Approach | Pros | Cons | When to use |
|----------|------|------|-------------|
| **`leaflet.vectorgrid` (L.VectorGrid.Protobuf)** | Pure Leaflet, no WebGL, works on very old browsers. | You have to style everything in JavaScript; no MapLibre style JSON support out-of-the-box. | Small projects, limited styling, need maximum compatibility. |
| **Render the whole map with MapLibre GL JS and embed Leaflet controls manually** | Full MapLibre feature set, no extra plugin. | You lose native Leaflet layers (markers, popups) unless you re-implement them. | When the map is *mostly* vector-tile based and you don’t need Leaflet’s layer system. |
| **Use `mapbox-gl-leaflet` (deprecated)** | Older tutorials use it. | Unmaintained, tied to Mapbox GL JS (now closed-source). | Legacy code that you can’t upgrade. |
| **Hybrid: raster fallback + GL for modern browsers** | Guarantees map works everywhere. | Requires two tile sources, extra bandwidth. | Public-facing sites where you must support very old devices. |

